  "worked_solution": "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x}-45\\cdot {5}^{x} & =6x\\cdot {5}^{x} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x}-6x\\cdot {5}^{x}-45\\cdot {5}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (3{x}^{2}-6x-45)\\cdot {5}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-6x-45=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $3{x}^{2}-6x-45=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(x+3)(x-5)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-3,5.$\n\n- The equation $3=0$ is not a possible solution because it's a false statement.\n\n- The equation ${5}^{x}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-3,5,$ and their product is $(-3)\\cdot 5=-15.$",
